    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d811">

                <pb facs="1006" n="882" sameAs="frus:frus1921v01/pg882"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">422.11 G 93/1158</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Ecuador</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Hartman</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Acting Secretary of
                            State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Quito</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1921-01-05"><hi rendition="#italic">January 5,
                        1921</hi></date>.<lb/>[Received February 1.]</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 634</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Referring to the Department’s telegram No.
                    41, of December 8,<anchor n="12" xml:id="d811-f1"/> relating to the interview
                    with the President of Ecuador recently published in the London <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Times</hi>, wherein the President is quoted as favoring
                    foreclosure of the Guayaquil &amp; Quito Railway by the British bondholders;
                    referring also to my telegrams No. 80, of December 13, 6 p.m., No. 81 of
                    December 16, 4 p.m., and No. 85 of December 23, 9 a.m.,<anchor n="13"
                        xml:id="d811-f2"/> and to my despatch No. 627 of December 21, 1920,<anchor
                        n="14" xml:id="d811-f3"/> I now have the honor to inform the Department that
                    I have received a note from the Minister for Foreign Affairs in answer to my
                    note to him, to which I made reference in the last paragraph of my said despatch
                    No. 627, and I am enclosing, for the information of the Department, triplicate
                    copies, with translation, of the Minister’s note, which is numbered 18, and
                    bears date December 29, 1920.</p>
                <p>I have [etc.]</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Chas. S. Hartman</hi></signed></closer>


                <xi:include href="d811-a1.xml" xpointer="d811-a1"/>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
